In the new API route `apps/web/pages/api/webhook/app-credential.ts`, the webhook secret is validated via a normal string inequality check:

```ts
if (req.headers[process.env.CALCOM_WEBHOOK_HEADER_NAME || "calcom-webhook-secret"] !== process.env.CALCOM_WEBHOOK_SECRET) {
  return res.status(403).json({ message: "Invalid webhook secret" });
}
```

This uses non-constant-time comparison (`!==`) between attacker-controlled input (header value) and a secret, which can theoretically leak information via timing differences. A constant-time comparison (e.g., `crypto.timingSafeEqual` after normalizing to buffers) is the standard mitigation for this class of issue. The finding is directly verifiable in the diff.

Yes. In `packages/app-store/_utils/oauth/parseRefreshTokenResponse.ts`, `minimumTokenResponseSchema` is declared as:
```ts
z.object({
  access_token: z.string(),
  [z.string().toString()]: z.number(),
  [z.string().optional().toString()]: z.unknown().optional(),
});
```
In JS/TS, computed object keys must evaluate to an actual string/symbol. Here `z.string()` returns a ZodString object; calling `.toString()` on it will produce a fixed string (very likely `"[object Object]"`), not “any string key”. That means the schema requires a literal property named `"[object Object]"` with a number value, which real OAuth token responses will not include, so `safeParse(response)` will fail and `parseRefreshTokenResponse` will throw `Invalid refreshed tokens were returned` when the “minimum” schema path is used.

(The “double parsing work” part is secondary, but the core claim—schema never matches real token responses due to the computed key—is a real, verifiable defect in the diff.)

In the new API route `apps/web/pages/api/webhook/app-credential.ts`, the webhook authentication check is implemented as a direct string inequality comparison:

```ts
if (
  req.headers[process.env.CALCOM_WEBHOOK_HEADER_NAME || "calcom-webhook-secret"] !==
  process.env.CALCOM_WEBHOOK_SECRET
) {
  return res.status(403).json({ message: "Invalid webhook secret" });
}
```

This uses normal JavaScript string comparison (`!==`), which is not constant-time. For secrets used to authenticate requests, best practice is a constant-time comparison (e.g., `crypto.timingSafeEqual`) to avoid timing side-channels. The finding is directly grounded in the diff and describes a real security weakness introduced by this code.

In `apps/web/pages/api/webhook/app-credential.ts`, the auth check is:

```ts
if (req.headers[process.env.CALCOM_WEBHOOK_HEADER_NAME || "calcom-webhook-secret"] !== process.env.CALCOM_WEBHOOK_SECRET)
  return 403;
```

This only compares equality and does not enforce that `CALCOM_WEBHOOK_SECRET` is set to a non-empty value. If `CALCOM_WEBHOOK_SECRET` is left as an empty string (as shown in `.env.example`) then an attacker can send the header with an empty value to satisfy the check (`"" !== ""` is false), allowing unauthorized requests. (Similarly, if the env var is unset and the header is missing, `undefined !== undefined` is also false.)

The new API route `apps/web/pages/api/webhook/app-credential.ts` authenticates requests by directly comparing a header value to `process.env.CALCOM_WEBHOOK_SECRET` using `!==`:

```ts
if (req.headers[process.env.CALCOM_WEBHOOK_HEADER_NAME || "calcom-webhook-secret"] !== process.env.CALCOM_WEBHOOK_SECRET) {
  return res.status(403).json({ message: "Invalid webhook secret" });
}
```

This is a normal JavaScript string comparison and is not constant-time, so it can (in principle) short-circuit and create a timing side-channel for the secret comparison. The finding is therefore grounded in the diff and describes a real security weakness introduced by this change (even if exploitation over the network may be difficult).
